  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "$\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{1}{t-2}=$",
  "worked_solution": "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $t(t-2),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $t-2$, and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{1}{t-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{t-2}{t-2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{t}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{t-2}{(t-2)t}-\\frac{t}{t(t-2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{t-2-t}{(t-2)t} & = \\\\ -\\frac{2}{(t-2)t} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
